mcs51 peephole: add the 'tail' rule modifier

A 'tail' rule is written between "replace" and '{', like "restart".
A normal rule replaces at the first match of a top-down scan; a tail
rule instead scans the whole block without replacing, remembers the
last position at which the pattern matched, and replaces only there.

This matters when match windows overlap.  The mcs51 rules 1012*
collapse two consecutive equivalent 'mov dptr' loads.  Given N such
loads in a row, a first-match rule rewrites the topmost pair and, even
with 'restart', keeps working at the top while the code between the
windows has already changed shape.  The reduction has to converge from
the bottom instead, folding the last reload into the one before it.
'tail' with 'restart' collapses one reload per pass, from the bottom,
until no match remains.

Implementation:
  - struct peepRule gains a tail:1 bit.
  - readRules replaces the old "look for 'restart' or '{'" scan with a
    loop that parses any rule flags before '{' (extensible for future
    flags); the flag is declared next to restart and barrier.
  - newPeepRule takes and stores the new flag.
  - The peepHole apply sequence (application counter, head/middle
    replaceRule, restart flag, per-match variable cleanup, value-trace
    release) is factored into a static helper applyRule, shared by the
    first-match and tail paths.  A tail rule only records the match
    position during the scan; after the scan the rule is re-matched and
    applied there.

Part of a series splitting a larger mcs51 peephole set into
independent pieces.

